/** @page ppc64le PowerPC64 little-endian (ppc64le) builds

@section ppc64le_linux Linux userspace unit tests

The @c ppc64le architecture supports two platforms: @c ieee1275 (the
default, described below) and @c linux.  The @c linux platform builds
iPXE as a 64-bit little-endian ELFv2 Linux executable.  This allows
the unit test suite to be run on POWER, or on any other host using
user-mode QEMU.

The build requires a @c powerpc64le-linux-gnu cross-compiler and C
library.  On Debian and Ubuntu these are provided by the packages @c
gcc-powerpc64le-linux-gnu and @c libc6-dev-ppc64el-cross, and the
emulator by the package @c qemu-user-static.

@code

	make -C src CROSS_COMPILE=powerpc64le-linux-gnu- bin-ppc64le-linux/tests.linux
	qemu-ppc64le-static -L /usr/powerpc64le-linux-gnu src/bin-ppc64le-linux/tests.linux

@endcode

The @c -L option names the directory containing the target dynamic
loader and C library, and is not required when running natively on
ppc64le.  A successful run ends with the line "OK: all N tests
passed".

@section ppc64le_ieee1275 Open Firmware (pSeries) image

The @c ieee1275 platform builds iPXE as a little-endian ELF image for
IEEE 1275 Open Firmware, as found on QEMU @c pseries (SLOF).  It is
the default platform for the @c ppc64le architecture.  The build needs
only the @c powerpc64le-linux-gnu cross-compiler:

@code

	make -C src CROSS_COMPILE=powerpc64le-linux-gnu- bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.elf

@endcode

The image is linked at a fixed address, so the firmware loads it
without relocation.  SLOF loads it from a CHRP boot CD, which the build
can produce directly.  Use @c EMBED to link a script into the image:

@code

	make -C src CROSS_COMPILE=powerpc64le-linux-gnu- bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.iso EMBED=script.ipxe
	qemu-system-ppc64 -M pseries -m 1G -nographic -cdrom src/bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.iso -boot d

@endcode

The ISO is plain ISO9660 with Rock Ridge extensions.  It holds the ELF
as @c /ppc/ipxe.elf and a @c /ppc/bootinfo.txt whose boot script
names the ELF with backslashes in the path (@c \\ppc\\ipxe.elf), the only
form SLOF reads.  The ISO is not bootable on BIOS or
EFI systems.  @c util/genfsimg rejects its @c -s script option with an ELF
input: link the script into the ELF with @c EMBED.  Building the ISO needs @c genisoimage, @c mkisofs
or @c xorrisofs.

iPXE prints its banner on the firmware console.  Press Ctrl-B at the
prompt to reach the iPXE shell.  Network access is described in @ref
ppc64le_ieee1275_net.

@subsection ppc64le_ieee1275_kernel Booting with the QEMU -kernel option

Passing the image to QEMU with @c -kernel does not work by default:
SLOF prints "Booting from memory..." and then raises a program
exception (vector 0x700) before iPXE produces any output.  The image
itself is not at fault.  QEMU does not load a @c -kernel ELF at its
physical address: it places the low 28 bits of each program header's
physical address at the machine's @c kernel-addr, which defaults to
0x400000.  An image linked at 0x400000 is therefore loaded at 0x800000
(QEMU reports "kernel_addr changed from 0x400000 to 0x800000"), and
SLOF enters it there.  The code is position-dependent, so it addresses
its data and zeroes its @c .bss at the link address, which overwrites
the running copy of the image.

Supporting the default load address would require a relocatable
image, which is not provided.  Setting @c kernel-addr to zero makes
QEMU load the image at its link address, and the image then boots to
the banner:

@code

	qemu-system-ppc64 -M pseries,kernel-addr=0 -m 1G -nographic \
		-kernel src/bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.elf

@endcode

@subsection ppc64le_ieee1275_memory Memory map and external heap

The system memory map is built from the memory nodes of the device
tree (the @c reg property gives the memory present, and the @c
available property the part that the firmware is not using).  The
firmware's client interface has 32-bit cells, so iPXE uses only memory
below 4GiB.

On first use the memory map provider claims the memory for the
external heap, and reports as usable only the memory that it has
claimed, so that @c umalloc() returns memory that the firmware knows to
be in use.  The claimed region is the largest free range below 4GiB
and above the iPXE image, less its lowest 128MiB (or half of the
range, if smaller), which is left unclaimed for the firmware's other
clients.  If the firmware refuses the claim there is no external heap,
and the shell still starts.

Building with @c DEBUG=ieee1275_memmap,uheap prints the free ranges,
the claimed region, and the resulting memory map.

@subsection ppc64le_ieee1275_net Network devices

Every node in the firmware device tree whose @c device_type is @c
network becomes an iPXE network device, with its MAC address taken from
the node's @c local-mac-address property.  Frames are sent and received
with the client interface @c write and @c read calls on an instance that
the device opens when it is opened and closes when it is closed, and
that is therefore also closed when iPXE shuts down.  The firmware
reports neither link state nor interrupts, so the link is always shown
as up and the device is polled.  Any adapter that the firmware can
boot from works, with no native driver.

On QEMU @c pseries, add a @c spapr-vlan NIC; repeat the option for
more than one:

@code

	qemu-system-ppc64 -M pseries -m 1G -nographic -cdrom src/bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.iso -boot d -nic user,model=spapr-vlan

@endcode

Building with @c DEBUG=ieee1275_net prints each device found, and each
open and close of its firmware instance.

@subsection ppc64le_ieee1275_rtas Time, reboot and power off

The time of day, @c reboot and @c poweroff come from RTAS (Run-Time
Abstraction Services), which the platform enables by default.  RTAS is
instantiated on first use: iPXE claims page-aligned memory below 4GiB
for the image, opens the @c /rtas node and calls its @c instantiate-rtas
method.  RTAS is entered through the same big-endian call thunk as the
client interface.  A missing or failing @c /rtas node is therefore an
error for the service that needs it and never prevents startup.

@c time() is the RTAS @c get-time-of-day value.  RTAS errors and dates
that are not plausible (before 2000, after 2200, or not a calendar
date) are never converted to a time: the clock then reads as the
seconds since startup, like the null time source, which precedes the
validity of any certificate.  The clock is otherwise trusted, as on other
platforms: a stale but plausible hardware clock is not detected, and
a failed read in the middle of a run steps the clock between real time
and the tick count.  Under
QEMU, set it with @c -rtc @c base=2031-05-17T08:09:10 and read it with
@c "echo ${unixtime}".

@c reboot calls @c system-reboot, and @c poweroff calls @c power-off.
Neither returns on success, and neither shuts iPXE down first.  Rebooting
to firmware setup is not supported.

The RTAS instance is closed and its memory released when iPXE shuts down,
whether it exits to the firmware or boots an operating system, so that the
next client can instantiate its own.  The release runs after every other
shutdown function, including the network devices.  Building with
@c DEBUG=ieee1275_rtas,ieee1275_rtas_time,ieee1275_rtas_reboot prints
the instantiation, each failure and the release.

@subsection ppc64le_ieee1275_boot Booting a Linux kernel

The @c kernel, @c initrd and @c boot commands start a little-endian
64-bit PowerPC ELF kernel, such as the @c vmlinuz of a distribution
installer (check with @c file that it is an ELF executable and not
compressed).  An image whose machine, class, byte order or file type is
not that of such a kernel is refused by @c kernel as not executable.
The image type makes any ppc64le ELF kernel fetched over the network
executable, so its authenticity is the operator's decision, as for any
other image (use HTTPS and image trust).  Every other registered image,
such as an @c initrd, is loaded as part
of the initrd, in order.  The kernel command line is the one given to
@c kernel (or set with @c imgargs).  For example:

@code

	dhcp
	kernel http://server/vmlinuz console=hvc0
	initrd http://server/initrd.img
	boot

@endcode

The kernel is loaded into memory that iPXE claims for it from the low
memory that the heap claim leaves unclaimed (see @ref
ppc64le_ieee1275_memory): at the first megabyte boundary above the iPXE
image, so never over the running image.  The size is taken from the
kernel's program headers, and the firmware refuses the claim if it
overlaps anything else.  A kernel started from the firmware claims
memory for itself below its real-mode limit (768MiB with the default
RMA), so it must not sit above that limit, however much RAM there is.
A relocatable kernel copies itself to physical address zero, so it does
not need to be loaded there.  The initrd is copied to the start of the
heap region, from the top of the region where the downloaded images are
squashed first; its end must be below their lowest address.  A kernel or
initrd that does not fit is refused before anything is shut down, and
the kernel's claim is then released.

Everything that can fail is done before iPXE shuts down: the kernel is
loaded and its instruction cache flushed, the layout is checked, and
the command line is stored in the @c bootargs property of @c /chosen.
Shutdown then closes the network devices (unless something holds @c
device_keep_count, as @c sanboot does) and releases RTAS, after which
the initrd is placed and the kernel is entered with the initrd address
and length in @c r3 and @c r4 and the firmware's client interface entry
point in @c r5, as the firmware itself would.  The last firmware call
releases the heap claim above the initrd, which the kernel needs in
order to claim memory for itself; the claims for the kernel and the
initrd stay.  A failed release is logged
and ignored.

Loading and placement are shared by all @c ppc64le platforms; the
platform primitives are @c ppc64le_boot_claim(), @c
ppc64le_boot_unclaim(), @c ppc64le_boot_prepare() and @c
ppc64le_boot_jump().  A platform that passes a flattened device tree
instead of a client interface supplies its own.  Only SLOF on QEMU has
been tested: PowerVM is not covered.  Building with @c DEBUG=elfboot,ieee1275_memmap,ieee1275_boot
prints the load addresses and the layout.

@section ppc64le_e2e Installer boot on QEMU

@c contrib/ppc64le/installer-menu.ipxe is an example embedded script
that sets a static IPv4 address (no DHCP), shows a menu and boots a
Fedora or Rocky Linux installer: @c kernel and @c initrd fetched over
HTTPS from the distribution mirror, and the mirror passed to the
installer as its repository.  The values that belong to QEMU (the user
networking address, netmask, gateway and DNS server, and the pseries
@c hvc0 console) are marked in the script.  Build and run it with:

@code

	make -C src CROSS_COMPILE=powerpc64le-linux-gnu- \
		bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.iso EMBED=../contrib/ppc64le/installer-menu.ipxe
	qemu-system-ppc64 -M pseries -cpu power9 -smp 4 -m 4G -nographic \
		-cdrom src/bin-ppc64le-ieee1275/ipxe.iso -boot d \
		-nic user,model=spapr-vlan

@endcode

Press @c f or @c r at the menu.  A POWER9 or later CPU, such as @c -cpu @c power9, is needed for the
entropy source (see @ref ppc64le_entropy), and 4GiB holds the largest
initrd, the kernel and the installer's own memory use.  The installers
need a few minutes under TCG.  If name lookups time out, the host's
resolver may not be usable through QEMU's DNS proxy at 10.0.2.3: set @c
net0/dns to a reachable server.

Both installers were run to their first interactive screen, the text
mode prompt of anaconda, and the console is recorded in
@c contrib/ppc64le/e2e-record.md.  That record also lists what was not
proven.

@subsection ppc64le_e2e_powernv Reuse by a PowerNV platform

A PowerNV platform (OPAL firmware, not part of this work) would reuse
the following unchanged, and add only what is listed last.

- R1, one ABI and firmware calls in one place.  The ELFv2 little-endian
  build (@c arch/ppc64le/Makefile, @c bits/ headers, @c core/setjmp.S) is
  shared by the @c linux and @c ieee1275 platforms.  The Open Firmware
  and RTAS calls are reached only through @c ppc64le_be_call() in @c
  ieee1275_thunk.S, called from @c ieee1275_call.c and @c
  ieee1275_rtas.c in @c arch/ppc64le/interface/ieee1275/.  OPAL would
  add its own thunk and call module beside them.
- R2, firmware-neutral architecture layer.  @c arch/ppc64le/core
  (@c timebase.c, @c darn.c, @c setjmp.S, @c rtas_date.c) and @c
  arch/ppc64le/image contain no firmware calls.  The timebase code needs
  only @c timebase_frequency() and the entropy source reads the
  processor version register.  Not neutral are the @c bits/time.h,
  @c bits/reboot.h and @c bits/memmap.h headers, which include the
  RTAS and Open Firmware provider macros, and @c rtas_date.c, which
  converts the RTAS date format; a PowerNV platform needs its own
  counterparts of these, and its own @c config/defaults file.
- R5, Linux hand-off in two parts.  The ELF kernel image type
  (@c image/elfboot.c) and the placement helpers (@c
  image/ppc64le_kernel.c) make no firmware calls.  The image type calls
  the four primitives declared in @c ipxe/ppc64le_kernel.h, implemented
  for Open Firmware in @c ieee1275_boot.c.  A PowerNV platform
  implements the same four, with a jump that passes a flattened device
  tree instead of the client interface entry point.  The tree would be
  built by @c fdt_create(), which needs a base tree in @c sysfdt (or an
  FDT image): nothing in @c arch/ppc64le fills @c sysfdt, so the
  platform would load the one that OPAL passes.  The placement helpers
  do carry one Open Firmware constraint: @c PPC64LE_KERNEL_LIMIT keeps
  the initrd region below 4GiB, the reach of the firmware's 32-bit
  cells, and a PowerNV platform would have to make it per-platform or
  accept it.  Where the kernel itself is placed is the platform's @c
  ppc64le_boot_claim().

@section ppc64le_entropy Entropy

The entropy source for both platforms is the @c DARN instruction,
introduced in Power ISA 3.0 (POWER9).  The source reads the processor
version register and reports itself unavailable, without executing
@c DARN, on earlier processors.  Under QEMU, @c -cpu power8 therefore
leaves the source unavailable, while @c -cpu power9 or later enables it.
On the @c linux platform, the source falls back to @c ENTROPY_LINUX
when @c DARN is unavailable.  Other processors have no hardware source
yet.

*/
